      "term": "optics-m4-elliptical-polarisation",
      "title": "E vector that turns and changes size",
      "meaning": "In elliptically polarised light the E vector changes size as it turns, and its tip traces an ellipse.",
      "points": [
        "Its magnitude varies with time while it rotates about the beam.",
        "Seen head-on, the tip of E traces an ellipse.",
        "In space, E sweeps a flattened helix."
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "info",
      "label": "05 · §20.5.3 Definition",
      "title": "What is elliptically polarised light?",
      "defines": [
        "optics-m4-elliptical-polarisation"
      ],
      "body": "Elliptically polarised light is light whose electric vector changes magnitude as it rotates, its tip tracing an ellipse.",
      "points": [
        "<b>Made of</b>: two coherent linear waves of different amplitudes, in perpendicular planes.",
        "<b>Phase</b>: out of phase by an angle θ; with unequal amplitudes, 90° is one such case.",
        "<b>Right or left</b>: set by the rotation seen by an observer looking back at the source."
      ],
